title: the probe test of a tending, step by step
pid: acc8c677-9169-43ab-9bc2-e14c190d6442
body: |2-
  For whoever runs it. Written 2026-11-13 by Gen; not yet run. Why it
  is built this way is in [[id:797628ed-c9fb-4a08-8922-d59eb854eed5][what I found on going back over a graph]].
  1. Pick a pair. In 'data/claude/team/NAME/notes', 'git log' shows a
     commit "..., as NAME left it" and then one "Tended: ...". Start
     with Vic's (4cc6854, then d273b28), where tending put a 330-line
     plan behind a link, and Libby's (878d3fe, then df4fc36), where it
     raised a general lesson above her one reading.
  2. Render what a waking mind reads at each commit: the home note drawn
     to every depth, as 'view' draws it. A scratch server on a checkout
     of the commit does it ("Running a scratch server for it" in
     'tools/skg-for-ai/README.org'); so would a short script following
     'contains' through 'git show COMMIT:ID.skg'. Leave link targets
     out, and note which probes needed one.
  3. Write the probes before anyone sees the tended version, by a mind
     that did not tend it and will not. It reads the graph as left, the
     transcripts of that specialist's missions, and the briefs of the
     missions after, which say what it came to need. Twenty to thirty
     probes, each with its answer and where the answer is, of five kinds:
     - a fact the graph holds that a later mission needed;
     - the limit of a principle, when its lesson does not apply
       (consolidation strips these first);
     - a superseded fact, where only the newer answer is right;
     - placement: where in the graph a note on a given thing would go
       (a third of the taste tests' misses were of this kind);
     - a question the graph cannot answer, whose right answer is "my
       notes do not say" (consolidation adds over-general claims and
       embellishment).
     Keep the probes in the tester's graph, never in the tested one, so
     no tender writes toward them.
  4. Answer: fresh minds given one rendering and the probes, nothing
     else; Haiku and Sonnet, three runs each per version, as the taste
     tests used. Run them also with no graph at all, and drop the probes
     a model answers unaided: those do not measure the graph.
  5. Grade with the key, by a mind that neither tended nor answered:
     each answer right, wrong, or "not said".
  6. Verdict. Better: more probes right after, no fact or limit probe
     going from right to wrong in most runs, and the rendering no
     longer. Only different: about the same scores and length. Worse:
     any probe right before and wrong after in most runs; each names
     what the tending lost, which is then put back.
  7. Report what the whole cost, so the next choice about tending can
     weigh it.
